        {
          "id": "24fca15a-3d5c-30ed-a310-fa5807b0e2e1",
          "content": {
            "id": "24fca15a-3d5c-30ed-a310-fa5807b0e2e1",
            "contentType": "VIDEO",
            "title": "Hot-diggity-dog: Smithfield Foods acquires iconic Nathan's Famous",
            "description": "<title><body><p>STORY: The most iconic hot dog brand in U.S. history will soon have a new owner.</p><p>The century-old Nathan's Famous is being gobbled up by Smithfield Foods in a deal valued at $450 million.</p><p>That's according to Smithfield, which will pay $102 a share, a nearly 10% premium to Nathan's close on Tuesday.</p><p>Nathan's stock jumped another 9% on Wednesday after the deal\u00a0was announced.</p><p>Shares of Smithfield, a majority-owned subsidiary of Chinese company WH Group, rose about 2%.\u00a0</p><p>Smithfield already holds an exclusive license to manufacture and sell Nathan's products in the U.S. and Canada and at Sam's Club stores in Mexico.</p><p>Nathan's Famous began as a hot dog stand in 1916, founded by immigrant Nathan Handwerker with $300 borrowed from entertainers Jimmy Durante and Eddie Cantor.</p><p>It initially sold hot dogs for 5 cents a piece.</p><p>The brand later expanded nationwide under the leadership of Handwerker's son, Murray.</p><p>Nathan's is known for its annual July 4th hot dog-eating contest in Coney Island, where winners are awarded a \"mustard belt\" for eating the most hot dogs.</p><p>Last year, winner Joey Chestnut - now a 17-time champ - devoured 70.5 hot dogs and buns in 10 minutes, according to the company's website.</p><p>The deal is expected to close in the first half of this year.</p></body></title>",
            "summary": "<body><p>STORY: The most iconic hot dog brand in U.S. history will soon have a new owner.</p><p>The century-old Nathan's Famous is being gobbled up by Smithfield Foods in a deal valued at $450 million.</p><p>That's according to Smithfield, which will pay $102 a share, a nearly 10% premium to Nathan's close on Tuesday.</p><p>Nathan's stock jumped another 9% on Wednesday after the deal&nbsp;was announced.</p><p>Shares of Smithfield, a majority-owned subsidiary of Chinese company WH Group, rose about 2%.&nbsp;</p><p>Smithfield already holds an exclusive license to manufacture and sell Nathan's products in the U.S. and Canada and at Sam's Club stores in Mexico.</p><p>Nathan's Famous began as a hot dog stand in 1916, founded by immigrant Nathan Handwerker with $300 borrowed from entertainers Jimmy Durante and Eddie Cantor.</p><p>It initially sold hot dogs for 5 cents a piece.</p><p>The brand later expanded nationwide under the leadership of Handwerker's son, Murray.</p><p>Nathan's is known for its annual July 4th hot dog-eating contest in Coney Island, where winners are awarded a \"mustard belt\" for eating the most hot dogs.</p><p>Last year, winner Joey Chestnut - now a 17-time champ - devoured 70.5 hot dogs and buns in 10 minutes, according to the company's website.</p><p>The deal is expected to close in the first half of this year.</p></body>",
